      {
        "id": "comment-title",
        "type": "comment",
        "position": {
          "x": 0.0,
          "y": 0.0
        },
        "data": {
          "text": "## Edge of Stability — Catapult Dynamics\n\n**Paper:** Cohen et al. 2021, *Gradient Descent on Neural Networks Typically Occurs at the Edge of Stability* ([arXiv:2103.00065](https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.00065))\n\n**Reproduced phenomenon:** With a fixed learning rate η, full-batch GD on a neural network:\n1. Initially reduces loss while sharpness λ_max *progressively rises*\n2. Once λ_max reaches **2/η** (the Edge-of-Stability threshold), sharpness *self-stabilises*\n3. Loss continues to decrease *non-monotonically* — periodic spikes (catapult oscillations)\n\n**Setup (easy / clean):**\n- Dataset: Synthetic regression — `linear_dataset`, inputDim=20, outputDim=1, 200 train samples, deterministic (no noise)\n- Model: 2-layer tanh MLP, width=32 (~1761 params) — small enough for **exact** Hessian\n- Optimizer: Pure SGD η=0.2, no momentum → **2/η = 10.0** (orange dashed line)\n- Loss: MSE — Hessian is always PSD, so power iteration reliably tracks the true λ_max\n- Full batch, every step logged (logFrequency=1) to capture each catapult oscillation\n\n**Why this setting is cleaner than MNIST + cross-entropy:**\n- MSE regression Hessian is positive semi-definite → no large negative eigenvalues\n- Exact Hessian (≤2048 params) gives ground-truth λ_max, no approximation artefacts\n- Deterministic targets → loss landscape is smooth and reproducible\n- 200 samples × 1761 params means the model is overparameterised but converges in ~500 steps"
        },
        "parentId": null,
        "extent": null,
        "hidden": null,
        "style": null
      },
      {
        "id": "hypothesis-eos",
        "type": "hypothesis",
        "position": {
          "x": 0.0,
          "y": 390.0
